    <!-- third section - more info -->
    <section class="section-3">
        <div class="page_container">
            <p class="ex">
                When calculating the relative angles, you can choose between the angles outside \( \pm 180 \) degrees
                or the one inside \( \pm 180 \) degrees.
                <br>
                <br> A bit of math trivia... Apply the complex conjugate to Euler’s famous formula (switch the sign of the imaginary part): $$Z = e^{iy} = cos(y) + i
                sin(y) \qquad \overline{Z} = e^{-ix} = cos(x) - isin(x).$$ Call \(x \) the conjugate angle and let \(x = 2 \pi - y\). We then have:
                $$\overline{Z} = e ^{- i ( 2\pi - y)} = e ^{-2\pi i} * e ^{iy} = cos(2\pi - y) - i(sin(2\pi - y))$$ \( Z \) is
                cyclical such that for \(n \in \mathbb{Z} \) $$ e ^{-in\pi} = \begin{cases} \space \space \space 1,&
                \text{n is even} \\ -1, & \text{n is odd} \end{cases} $$ With the trigonometric identities:
                $$cos(x) = cos(2\pi - y) = cos(-y) = cos(y) \qquad sin(x) = sin(2\pi - y) = sin(-y) = -
                sin(y) $$ We get: $$ e^{iy} = cos(y) + i sin (y) = cos(2\pi - y) - i(sin(2\pi - y)) = e^{-ix} $$ 
                Meaning that the complex conjugate is the same as phase shifting to the negative conjugate angle, hence the etymology.
            </p>
        </div>
